{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggrJgUj95j3NbyfB7FyW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For high-spin octahedral d⁷, I get **Dq ≈ 1037 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 822 cm⁻¹**, with the 19 550 cm⁻¹ band assigned to ⁴A₂g and the 20 320 cm⁻¹ band to ⁴T₁g(P). I worked this out by hand and did not use the tools.\n\n**Model**\n\nAssume a high-spin octahedral d⁷ ion (Co²⁺-like) with a ⁴T₁g(F) ground state. Its three spin-allowed bands are ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₂g, → ⁴A₂g and → ⁴T₁g(P).\n\nMeasured from the ⁴F term, the quartet energies are:\n- ⁴T₂g = +2Dq\n- ⁴A₂g = +12Dq\n- The two ⁴T₁g levels are the roots of the 2×2 matrix [[−6Dq, 4Dq], [4Dq, 15B]].\n\nThese give three relations:\n- ν(⁴A₂g) − ν(⁴T₂g) = 10Dq\n- Sum of the two ⁴T₁g roots = 15B − 6Dq\n- Product of the two ⁴T₁g roots = −90DqB − 16Dq²\n\n**Fitting the bands**\n\nν₁ = 9 180 cm⁻¹ is clearly ⁴T₂g. The two upper bands are close together, so both assignments need checking.\n\n- **Option A: 19 550 = ⁴A₂g and 20 320 = ⁴T₁g(P).**\n  - 10Dq = 19 550 − 9 180 = 10 370, so Dq = 1037 cm⁻¹.\n  - The ground level is E(⁴T₁g(F)) = 2Dq − ν₁ = −7 106 cm⁻¹, so E(⁴T₁g(P)) = −7 106 + 20 320 = 13 214 cm⁻¹.\n  - From the sum: 15B − 6Dq = 6 108, so B = 822 cm⁻¹.\n  - Check with the product: (−7 106)(13 214) = −9.390×10⁷, and −90DqB − 16Dq² = −9.392×10⁷. These agree to 0.03%, so this assignment is consistent.\n- **Option B: 20 320 = ⁴A₂g.**\n  - This gives Dq = 1114 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 822 cm⁻¹ from the sum.\n  - The product check fails: −8.76×10⁷ against −1.02×10⁸. This assignment is rejected.\n\n**Result**\n\n- Dq ≈ 1037 cm⁻¹, so 10Dq ≈ 10 370 cm⁻¹.\n- B ≈ 822 cm⁻¹. For Co²⁺ (free-ion B ≈ 1115 cm⁻¹), this would mean β ≈ 0.74; the ion is not named in the question.\n- C cannot be found from these data. The spin-allowed quartet energies of d⁷ depend only on Dq and B, so C is left as null.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1037, \"B\": 822, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"High-spin d7 quartet band